      ":question/id": "d024fc1d-fd3e-501a-a84d-5d86c65a6cf2",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=-1 \\\\ y+z &=-2 \\\\ z &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=-2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=-2-(-2)=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=0$ and $z=-2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(0)-(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\n$$\nx=-1-(0)-(2)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-3$, $y=0$, and $z=-2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
